1
00:00:07,670 --> 00:00:09,400
<i>I've gone and done it again.</i>

2
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:11,000
[RINKA: Where are you?]

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,210
<i>I really am unlucky.</i>
[RINKA: Don't tell me you forgot]

4
00:00:14,210 --> 00:00:16,630
Are you listening, Mr. Ichinose?

5
00:00:16,630 --> 00:00:18,990
Of course. My apologies.

6
00:00:18,990 --> 00:00:20,940
An apology won't cut it.

7
00:00:20,940 --> 00:00:22,980
Ma'am, please listen.

8
00:00:23,670 --> 00:00:27,700
Kubota, you put Miyagi in a headlock.

9
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:30,690
It was the other child who was in the wrong.

10
00:00:30,690 --> 00:00:33,090
Akito said he was betrayed!

11
00:00:33,090 --> 00:00:35,980
He was, in a game.

12
00:00:36,980 --> 00:00:40,010
Their team lost a match in an online shooter.

13
00:00:40,010 --> 00:00:41,930
Because he sucked!

14
00:00:41,930 --> 00:00:46,230
Ma'am, could we ask you to put a stop
to his gaming at night?

15
00:00:46,230 --> 00:00:48,790
A lack of sleep affects his studies, and...

16
00:00:48,790 --> 00:00:52,790
What? You're telling me I'm accountable?

17
00:00:52,790 --> 00:00:57,280
Matters that occur at school
are the teacher's responsibility!

18
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:00,400
However, we cannot monitor
how your children spend their nights.

19
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:02,990
And why is my child the only one apologizing?

20
00:01:02,990 --> 00:01:04,830
Both children engaged in the fight.

21
00:01:04,830 --> 00:01:08,820
Teachers like you
always side with the victims.

22
00:01:18,510 --> 00:01:20,460
Mr. Ichinose.

23
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,410
I'm terribly sorry, ma'am.

24
00:01:31,370 --> 00:01:33,690
<i>I'm an unlucky guy.</i>

25
00:01:33,690 --> 00:01:35,680
Ma'am...

26
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:42,010
Come on...

27
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:44,140
Go, go!

28
00:01:44,140 --> 00:01:47,790
You almost got it!

29
00:01:47,790 --> 00:01:49,680
[TIME UP]

30
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:54,150
<i>I'm confident I'm the unluckiest dude around.</i>

31
00:01:54,150 --> 00:01:56,350
<i>Shoot, I forgot about Rinka.</i>

32
00:01:56,350 --> 00:01:58,320
[RINKA: Don't tell me you forgot. Hey!]

33
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:01,090
<i>At my previous schools...</i>

34
00:02:01,090 --> 00:02:03,850
I understand this is your grandma's memento,

35
00:02:03,850 --> 00:02:06,680
but you don't have to
bring it to school, right?

36
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:07,620
Yeah.

37
00:02:07,620 --> 00:02:10,860
I'll hold onto it,
so come get it after school.

38
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:12,240
Okay.

39
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:14,800
- Mr. Ichinose.
- Yes?

40
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:17,650
A student from Class 2 came to get something.

41
00:02:17,650 --> 00:02:20,590
I'll be right there.

42
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:26,170
I just put it here.

43
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:27,900
Where did it go?

44
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:30,860
[Apology Regarding the Lost Brooch]

45
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:33,510
Mr. Ichinose, please stop them!

46
00:02:33,510 --> 00:02:37,040
What are you two doing? Cut it out!

47
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:38,550
I said, cut it out!

48
00:02:38,550 --> 00:02:41,140
- Ouch!
- He hit her!

49
00:02:41,140 --> 00:02:43,630
- Are you okay? Where did you get hit?
- Don't touch me!

50
00:02:43,630 --> 00:02:45,150
- Are you okay?
- He touched her.

51
00:02:45,150 --> 00:02:47,420
He touched her!

52
00:02:47,420 --> 00:02:49,740
- He touched her!
- It wasn't on purpose!

53
00:02:49,740 --> 00:02:53,990
You hurt my child, and touched her.

54
00:02:53,990 --> 00:02:56,500
It is a misunderstanding.

55
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:58,880
But it's true you touched her, no?

56
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:03,020
My daughter says she is traumatized.

57
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:12,700
I'm terribly sorry for what I've caused.

58
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:15,840
[Apology Regarding Unwarranted Contact]

59
00:03:20,910 --> 00:03:23,140
<i>Mr. Ichinose stole it!</i>

60
00:03:23,140 --> 00:03:25,130
<i>She refuses to go to school because of you!</i>

61
00:03:25,130 --> 00:03:27,300
- <i>He assaulted her.</i>
- <i>He created disciplinary issues!</i>

62
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:28,650
<i>How come you punished her?</i>

63
00:03:28,650 --> 00:03:32,150
- <i>He touched her!</i>
- <i>Take accountability!</i>

64
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:38,070
Mr. Ichinose.
How about considering a transfer?

65
00:03:38,070 --> 00:03:40,240
I'm sure it's getting difficult to stay.

66
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:46,020
[The wild Blissey ran away!]

67
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,470
<i>I've given up all ridiculous expectations.</i>

68
00:03:50,470 --> 00:03:53,430
<i>My sole goal at this new school, is...</i>

69
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:56,720
[Inoffensive]

70
00:03:57,490 --> 00:03:59,200
To be inoffensive!

71
00:04:03,370 --> 00:04:04,950
Time to go home.

72
00:04:14,170 --> 00:04:17,580
Woah, who left this here?

73
00:04:17,580 --> 00:04:19,560
Watch out!

74
00:04:21,390 --> 00:04:23,560
I'm sorry, Ms. Nashiki!

75
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:26,690
- Are you alright? I'm so sorry!
- It's fine.

76
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:31,630
I'm sorry, it's my fault
for being in the way.

77
00:04:47,250 --> 00:04:49,780
Man, I really am unlucky today.

78
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:54,360
But I guess my luck has always been terrible.

79
00:04:54,360 --> 00:04:56,800
I caused so much trouble
at my last school, too.

80
00:04:57,570 --> 00:05:00,160
Like that one time I forgot
to turn off the pool pump.

81
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:01,800
What?

82
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:07,010
The weather took a turn,
so pool day was canceled.

83
00:05:07,010 --> 00:05:11,820
Then one of my students got a fever.
And it was a Friday.

84
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:15,050
- Really, there was a lot going on.
- You left it running?

85
00:05:15,050 --> 00:05:16,470
Until Monday?

86
00:05:16,470 --> 00:05:20,820
Yeah. The water bill was over a million yen.

87
00:05:21,350 --> 00:05:22,740
A million...

88
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:28,560
The city took on half the bill.
The rest, I split with the Principal.

89
00:05:29,150 --> 00:05:33,640
Still, paying out of pocket
for a mistake at school... It sucked.

90
00:05:35,540 --> 00:05:39,150
Anyway, the Principal hated me after that,

91
00:05:39,150 --> 00:05:41,000
which is why I'm here now.

92
00:05:44,670 --> 00:05:49,160
You never once thought about quitting?

93
00:05:50,320 --> 00:05:54,900
I admit I've considered it.

94
00:05:56,510 --> 00:05:58,500
But, you know.

95
00:06:01,850 --> 00:06:05,810
You're very tough, Mr. Ichinose.

96
00:06:06,620 --> 00:06:08,420
You think?

97
00:06:10,580 --> 00:06:12,760
I get beaten down all the time though.

98
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:17,680
- Here.
- Thank you.

99
00:06:19,470 --> 00:06:22,210
Today sure was another rough day.

100
00:06:22,210 --> 00:06:25,930
Parents are complaining, and it's only April.

101
00:06:25,930 --> 00:06:31,250
But you don't give in.

102
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:33,840
Well...

103
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:38,370
Thanks to you, I could smile again
for the first time today.

104
00:06:39,150 --> 00:06:41,070
See you after the break.

105
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:44,910
Wait.

106
00:06:46,530 --> 00:06:48,760
Are you heading back to the classroom?

107
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:51,920
Yes. I still have work to finish.

108
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:53,630
Sorry.

109
00:06:54,800 --> 00:07:01,640
<i>Iroha Nashiki. Another new hire
who started this April like me.</i>

110
00:07:02,810 --> 00:07:04,890
<i>At the time, I had no idea</i>

111
00:07:04,890 --> 00:07:10,160
<i>she had spent the last year
away from work, depressed.</i>

112
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:27,600
Mr. Ichinose, over here.

113
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:34,140
Good evening.

114
00:07:34,140 --> 00:07:35,520
Thanks for coming.

115
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:38,550
Is this everyone
from Third Elementary School?

116
00:07:38,550 --> 00:07:43,610
A teacher named Nashiki was to join us,
but we can proceed without her.

117
00:07:43,610 --> 00:07:46,450
- I see. Then let's get started.
- Right.

118
00:07:46,450 --> 00:07:52,550
Folks, we appreciate your help in patrolling
Shibuya to look out for our youth.

119
00:07:56,690 --> 00:07:58,750
I wonder where Ms. Nashiki could be.

120
00:07:58,750 --> 00:08:00,590
She didn't show, and that's that.

121
00:08:00,590 --> 00:08:02,370
Mr. Ichinose, the flag.

122
00:08:02,370 --> 00:08:03,930
Right.

123
00:08:39,320 --> 00:08:41,770
Hey, what're you guys doing out this late?

124
00:08:41,770 --> 00:08:43,940
Don't worry, I got this.

125
00:08:43,940 --> 00:08:45,150
You're high schoolers, right?

126
00:08:45,150 --> 00:08:46,720
Who is this man?

127
00:08:46,720 --> 00:08:48,800
- My father. Right?
- Y-Yes.

128
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:50,040
See?

129
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:51,790
Your "father", is it?

130
00:08:51,790 --> 00:08:54,470
- What's the problem?
- I doubt it.

131
00:09:07,860 --> 00:09:12,080
Hey, aren't you guys elementary schoolers?

132
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:13,510
It's already way past 8PM.

133
00:09:13,510 --> 00:09:18,320
<i>Tonight, a comet last visible
341 years ago in 1685,</i>

134
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:20,980
<i>will grace our skies once more.</i>

135
00:09:21,910 --> 00:09:24,800
<i>This shot was taken this morning at 5AM,</i>

136
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:28,110
<i>using four different cameras across Tokyo.</i>

137
00:09:28,110 --> 00:09:30,960
<i>Zoom into the northern sky,
and you will see...</i>

138
00:09:30,960 --> 00:09:32,860
Ready?

139
00:09:33,900 --> 00:09:35,910
What are you doing?

140
00:09:36,730 --> 00:09:38,590
They said they'd go home if I joined...

141
00:09:38,590 --> 00:09:40,060
Get out.

142
00:09:40,060 --> 00:09:42,230
- Aw, come on.
- I'm calling your parents.

143
00:09:42,230 --> 00:09:44,530
- Let us have our picture!
- No.

144
00:09:44,530 --> 00:09:47,100
- Why not?
- Do you realize what time it is?

145
00:09:50,370 --> 00:09:53,820
- Why didn't you scold them?
- <i>Five, four,</i>

146
00:09:55,250 --> 00:09:57,000
- Sorry.
- <i>three, two, one!</i>

147
00:09:59,140 --> 00:10:00,910
Nice photo.

148
00:10:00,910 --> 00:10:03,370
- We're leaving.
- Right.

149
00:10:41,850 --> 00:10:44,900
I pray nothing eventful happens tomorrow.

150
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:53,180
<i>To be inoffensive is the safest option.</i>

151
00:10:53,180 --> 00:10:56,140
<i>I want to avoid conflict at all costs.</i>

152
00:10:56,140 --> 00:11:02,950
<i>We will be seeing the same sky
as our Edo ancestors, 341 years ago.</i>

153
00:11:02,950 --> 00:11:07,490
- A comet.
- <i>The Knox Comet, visible every 120 years.</i>

154
00:11:07,490 --> 00:11:12,960
<i>Though tonight is a cloudy night,
perhaps we can see the comet peek through.</i>

155
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:16,740
<i>Now, for our next headline.</i>

156
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,570
Seriously?

157
00:11:30,570 --> 00:11:33,140
This can't be happening.

158
00:11:39,940 --> 00:11:42,580
Damn it!

159
00:12:04,850 --> 00:12:06,650
Please, wait!

160
00:12:08,180 --> 00:12:09,710
Calm down.

161
00:12:09,710 --> 00:12:12,840
I'll be right over there.

162
00:12:23,600 --> 00:12:25,410
Ms. Nashiki?

163
00:12:26,450 --> 00:12:29,020
That's you, isn't it?

164
00:12:29,020 --> 00:12:30,260
But why?

165
00:12:30,260 --> 00:12:33,680
- Mr. Ichinose...
- No! Don't move!

166
00:12:34,690 --> 00:12:35,840
Don't move.

167
00:12:35,840 --> 00:12:38,460
I'll be right over there.

168
00:12:42,080 --> 00:12:45,200
Ms. Nashiki, your hand.

169
00:13:08,330 --> 00:13:11,970
<i>God! I don't care if I'm unlucky for life!</i>

170
00:13:11,970 --> 00:13:15,190
<i>Just please, give me this one chance!</i>

171
00:13:31,320 --> 00:13:34,250
Ms. Nashiki! Your hand!

172
00:13:59,490 --> 00:14:01,220
She's alive.

173
00:14:09,560 --> 00:14:11,500
Please, wait!

174
00:14:16,420 --> 00:14:18,420
Where the hell am I?

175
00:14:19,350 --> 00:14:21,420
Ms. Nashiki?

176
00:14:21,420 --> 00:14:25,460
Is this paradise?

177
00:14:25,460 --> 00:14:27,250
What?

178
00:14:27,250 --> 00:14:29,360
Ms. Nashiki, did you hit your head somewhere?

179
00:14:31,590 --> 00:14:33,520
Who the hell are you?

180
00:14:33,520 --> 00:14:35,220
- What?
- State your name!

181
00:14:35,220 --> 00:14:37,940
I'm your coworker, Ikuru Ichinose.

182
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:39,670
What's wrong?

183
00:14:39,670 --> 00:14:42,210
And why are you talking like that?

184
00:14:42,210 --> 00:14:44,600
The hell's going on?

185
00:14:44,600 --> 00:14:48,490
Where the hell am I?

186
00:14:48,490 --> 00:14:50,960
Is this an illusion?

187
00:14:50,960 --> 00:14:52,400
Ms. Nashiki?

188
00:14:55,400 --> 00:14:59,430
Screw off!

189
00:15:09,270 --> 00:15:12,710
[Episode 1]

190
00:15:14,310 --> 00:15:18,110
Excuse me, I'm not exactly following...

191
00:15:19,680 --> 00:15:21,110
Should I call an ambulance?

192
00:15:21,110 --> 00:15:23,240
The hell are you talking about?

193
00:15:23,980 --> 00:15:26,730
Wait, where's my plaque?

194
00:15:26,730 --> 00:15:27,600
Huh?

195
00:15:27,600 --> 00:15:30,060
Master Seki's geometry plaque!

196
00:15:30,060 --> 00:15:32,820
I was just looking at it!

197
00:15:32,820 --> 00:15:35,390
You weren't looking at anything.

198
00:15:35,390 --> 00:15:37,650
You were trying to jump.

199
00:15:37,650 --> 00:15:39,000
Hands off!

200
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:40,640
Sorry!

201
00:15:41,420 --> 00:15:44,260
What happened to you?

202
00:15:44,260 --> 00:15:46,840
- It's me, Ichinose.
- The hell's your problem?

203
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:49,240
Creep.

204
00:15:50,330 --> 00:15:52,310
Sorry.

205
00:15:52,310 --> 00:15:56,320
How dare you claim a surname
when you're no warrior?

206
00:15:56,320 --> 00:15:59,750
And that odd attire.

207
00:15:59,750 --> 00:16:02,030
Quit acting so familiar with me!

208
00:16:02,030 --> 00:16:03,570
Sorry.

209
00:16:05,480 --> 00:16:09,670
The hell am I wearing?

210
00:16:09,670 --> 00:16:13,550
It feels too breezy.

211
00:16:13,550 --> 00:16:15,050
Do not take it off!

212
00:16:15,050 --> 00:16:18,220
Where's my kimono? And my netsuke?

213
00:16:18,220 --> 00:16:20,320
You weren't wearing a kimono!

214
00:16:20,320 --> 00:16:21,860
This isn't the Edo period.

215
00:16:21,860 --> 00:16:23,920
Edo period? What's that?

216
00:16:23,920 --> 00:16:25,680
It's the period of Edo, what else?

217
00:16:25,680 --> 00:16:27,600
Aren't you a certified teacher, Ms. Nashiki?

218
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:30,450
Who's this "Ms. Nashiki"
you keep talking about?

219
00:16:32,520 --> 00:16:36,350
You. You, Iroha Nashiki.

220
00:16:38,940 --> 00:16:40,990
Quit your ridiculous talk.

221
00:16:40,990 --> 00:16:43,790
I'm no warrior. I have no surname!

222
00:16:43,790 --> 00:16:45,430
- What?
- Listen well.

223
00:16:45,430 --> 00:16:48,490
I come from a family of couriers.

224
00:16:48,490 --> 00:16:51,900
"A great mountain road intersects
the Shibuya and Uda rivers."

225
00:16:51,900 --> 00:16:56,330
"Delivering from Edo's Akasaka to Isehara,

226
00:16:56,330 --> 00:17:01,900
people call us, the Tokubei Couriers!"

227
00:17:04,410 --> 00:17:06,460
You sound like you're from the Edo period.

228
00:17:06,460 --> 00:17:09,790
- And what's that?
- The period of Edo!

229
00:17:09,790 --> 00:17:11,900
The one before Meiji?
Tell me you've heard of Tokugawa.

230
00:17:11,900 --> 00:17:14,730
'Course I know the Tokugawa Family!

231
00:17:14,730 --> 00:17:18,740
Lord Tsunayoshi became the 5th shogun
just the other day.

232
00:17:18,740 --> 00:17:21,540
Tsunayoshi.
Known for instituting animal welfare laws.

233
00:17:21,540 --> 00:17:24,190
The year, 1685.
[1685 - The Rescued Hachiko]

234
00:17:24,190 --> 00:17:27,790
1685? That's 341 years ago!

235
00:17:27,790 --> 00:17:29,780
Hey, what gives?

236
00:17:30,520 --> 00:17:32,980
I refuse to accept this is happening.

237
00:17:32,980 --> 00:17:35,520
In other words...

238
00:17:35,520 --> 00:17:39,500
<i>We will be seeing the same sky
as our Edo ancestors.</i>

239
00:17:39,500 --> 00:17:41,460
The comet?

240
00:17:45,130 --> 00:17:49,280
<i>No, calm down.
This is your unluckiest incident yet.</i>

241
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:52,620
This really does look like paradise.

242
00:17:52,620 --> 00:17:55,980
<i>It's like I was born to be unlucky.</i>

243
00:17:55,980 --> 00:18:00,020
<i>But why is she speaking
like she's from 341 years ago?</i>

244
00:18:00,650 --> 00:18:03,720
Hey, are you alright?

245
00:18:03,720 --> 00:18:06,610
<i>Can it be? Did she really time travel...</i>

246
00:18:06,610 --> 00:18:09,270
No, no, no!

247
00:18:09,270 --> 00:18:12,460
I'm not falling for your tricks, Ms. Nashiki.

248
00:18:12,460 --> 00:18:13,580
Huh?

249
00:18:13,580 --> 00:18:17,280
There are still other possibilities.

250
00:18:17,280 --> 00:18:20,270
Let's explore our most realistic options.

251
00:18:20,270 --> 00:18:22,800
Possibilities?

252
00:18:23,950 --> 00:18:26,500
- Hey!
- Sorry, can I look through this?

253
00:18:26,500 --> 00:18:28,610
- Huh?
- Okay, I am.

254
00:18:32,710 --> 00:18:34,670
"Iroha Nashiki".

255
00:18:34,670 --> 00:18:38,830
See, look. This is your face, isn't it?

256
00:18:40,930 --> 00:18:42,870
My face?

257
00:18:45,800 --> 00:18:47,480
Do I need to repeat myself?

258
00:18:47,480 --> 00:18:52,100
I'm Iroha, daughter to the Tokubei Couriers.
I have no surname!

259
00:18:53,740 --> 00:18:55,370
Okay, now I'm sure.

260
00:18:56,270 --> 00:19:00,950
I knew we were both
heading towards the deep end.

261
00:19:00,950 --> 00:19:07,430
Though I know you both went through
some troubles at your previous schools,

262
00:19:07,430 --> 00:19:12,990
I hope this school will provide
a fresh start for you both.

263
00:19:16,240 --> 00:19:18,950
- Looking forward to your guidance.
- Pleased to meet you all.

264
00:19:18,950 --> 00:19:23,400
Ms. Nashiki. Louder, if you could.

265
00:19:24,750 --> 00:19:27,280
Pleased to meet you all.

266
00:19:27,280 --> 00:19:30,090
<i>We've both finally lost it.</i>

267
00:19:30,090 --> 00:19:32,960
- Huh?
- Didn't you feel that odd sensation earlier?

268
00:19:32,960 --> 00:19:36,720
It was like space warped
so I could catch you.

269
00:19:38,120 --> 00:19:43,530
I don't even know why I'm here.
It's like I'm trapped in an illusion.

270
00:19:43,530 --> 00:19:45,410
What a nightmare.

271
00:19:45,410 --> 00:19:51,090
Exactly, that feeling of a dream.
It means we're avoiding reality.

272
00:19:51,090 --> 00:19:55,140
Either way, the cause of all this is stress.

273
00:19:55,140 --> 00:19:57,450
- Stress?
- Yeah.

274
00:19:58,060 --> 00:20:02,410
And right after I found another school...

275
00:20:02,410 --> 00:20:03,740
Guess I'll go see a therapist.

276
00:20:03,740 --> 00:20:08,040
Tell me, how do I wake up from this dream?

277
00:20:08,040 --> 00:20:10,660
How do I go back home?

278
00:20:15,110 --> 00:20:18,680
Get in. Or else you can't go down.

279
00:20:26,740 --> 00:20:31,250
<i>I hope this is just a nightmare.</i>

280
00:20:35,640 --> 00:20:37,310
You shut us in here!

281
00:20:37,310 --> 00:20:39,650
Bastard!

282
00:20:41,400 --> 00:20:43,160
The hell?

283
00:20:43,160 --> 00:20:44,270
What's going on?

284
00:20:44,270 --> 00:20:48,260
<i>I mean, what am I supposed to do?</i>

285
00:20:55,520 --> 00:20:57,730
What are you doing?

286
00:21:01,340 --> 00:21:03,850
You're shaking, too.

287
00:21:12,110 --> 00:21:18,270
<i>No matter how unlucky I may be,
this is simply unheard of.</i>

288
00:21:21,530 --> 00:21:23,010
Ms. Nashiki.

289
00:21:24,720 --> 00:21:27,070
What in the world?

290
00:21:32,750 --> 00:21:34,000
Is that a monster?

291
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:35,420
Ms. Nashiki!

292
00:21:35,420 --> 00:21:36,880
Sorry!

293
00:21:42,990 --> 00:21:45,910
You okay? Are you drunk?

294
00:21:45,910 --> 00:21:48,070
Hands off, you bastard!

295
00:21:48,070 --> 00:21:50,680
Ms. Nashiki! Stop that!

296
00:21:51,520 --> 00:21:53,340
- Keep her in check!
- Sorry! She's drunk!

297
00:21:53,340 --> 00:21:55,440
- Crazy chick!
- I'm not drunk!

298
00:21:55,440 --> 00:21:56,850
Calm down, please!

299
00:21:56,850 --> 00:21:58,770
I'll call for a taxi right away.

300
00:21:58,770 --> 00:22:01,520
- Taxi!
- Wait.

301
00:22:02,550 --> 00:22:07,070
I can trust you, right?

302
00:22:07,760 --> 00:22:13,540
Yes. At the very least,
I'm acting with your best interest in mind.

303
00:22:19,530 --> 00:22:23,500
So bright at this time of night...

304
00:22:23,500 --> 00:22:26,430
This really is like a dream.

305
00:22:29,940 --> 00:22:34,470
Is this place like the Dragon Palace?

306
00:22:34,470 --> 00:22:38,970
The Dragon Palace? From Urashima Taro?

307
00:22:39,840 --> 00:22:41,880
Woah.

308
00:22:41,880 --> 00:22:44,810
<i>This place is no paradise like that.</i>

309
00:22:44,810 --> 00:22:48,360
Wish I could tell Ma and Pa about this.

310
00:22:48,360 --> 00:22:51,140
So long as 300 years don't pass

311
00:22:51,140 --> 00:22:56,760
while I'm gone like Urashima Taro though.

312
00:22:56,760 --> 00:23:00,240
<i>Sorry, you've actually skipped 341 years.</i>

313
00:23:01,200 --> 00:23:08,550
<i>Hold on. If she really is
someone else right now,</i>

314
00:23:10,340 --> 00:23:14,640
<i>where is this Iroha Nashiki?</i>

315
00:23:18,380 --> 00:23:20,030
Thank you.

316
00:23:20,570 --> 00:23:21,960
- Do you need a receipt?
- No, thank you.

317
00:23:21,960 --> 00:23:24,400
- Of course. Get back safely.
- Where is this place?

318
00:23:25,070 --> 00:23:27,200
- I can't get out!
- Hold on.

319
00:23:33,350 --> 00:23:35,790
This is supposedly your home, Ms. Nashiki.

320
00:23:35,790 --> 00:23:39,090
The hell is that?

321
00:23:39,090 --> 00:23:40,920
Tell me, what is it?

322
00:23:44,150 --> 00:23:45,770
This is your room.

323
00:23:48,170 --> 00:23:49,880
Here, your keys.

324
00:23:49,880 --> 00:23:52,210
Huh? What's this?

325
00:23:52,210 --> 00:23:54,190
Do you not lock up behind you?

326
00:23:54,190 --> 00:23:57,710
'Course I do. With my trusty door bar.

327
00:23:57,710 --> 00:24:00,550
Well, think of this as the modern "door bar".

328
00:24:00,550 --> 00:24:05,100
How's that supposed to...

329
00:24:05,100 --> 00:24:06,500
Huh?

330
00:24:07,470 --> 00:24:08,960
Go on.

331
00:24:13,300 --> 00:24:15,320
Please excuse me.

332
00:24:22,690 --> 00:24:25,940
It stinks in here.

333
00:24:27,350 --> 00:24:29,040
Remember anything?

334
00:24:29,040 --> 00:24:30,450
No.

335
00:24:31,450 --> 00:24:35,620
Well now, what to do...

336
00:24:37,540 --> 00:24:41,000
This turns the light on and off.

337
00:24:49,650 --> 00:24:52,530
- Stop that.
- Sorry.

338
00:24:53,180 --> 00:24:56,610
Since it'd be a ruckus
taking this out at night,

339
00:24:56,610 --> 00:24:58,440
take this garbage to the lot in the morning.

340
00:24:58,440 --> 00:25:00,890
- The lot?
- Yes.

341
00:25:02,100 --> 00:25:03,750
Sorry in advance.

342
00:25:06,320 --> 00:25:08,770
This seems to be your bath room.

343
00:25:10,670 --> 00:25:11,770
Take your shoes off!

344
00:25:11,770 --> 00:25:14,740
I thought we had dirt floors!

345
00:25:20,600 --> 00:25:22,240
This is your restroom.

346
00:25:22,240 --> 00:25:25,360
The wash closet, if you will.

347
00:25:25,360 --> 00:25:26,880
Turn this, and it'll flush.

348
00:25:26,880 --> 00:25:29,600
Woah!

349
00:25:29,600 --> 00:25:32,850
It produces water without going to a well?

350
00:25:32,850 --> 00:25:34,960
You have hot water, too. Here's the boiler.

351
00:25:34,960 --> 00:25:37,190
Hot water?

352
00:25:37,190 --> 00:25:39,640
Give it a whirl later.

353
00:25:39,640 --> 00:25:42,250
- I'll continue with my explanation.
- Alright.

354
00:25:42,250 --> 00:25:44,000
This is your kitchen.

355
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,340
You'll do your cooking here.

356
00:25:47,410 --> 00:25:49,210
And this is how you get fire.

357
00:25:50,530 --> 00:25:52,180
How did you do that?

358
00:25:53,070 --> 00:25:54,650
You press here.

359
00:26:00,340 --> 00:26:05,370
This has to be magic.

360
00:26:06,150 --> 00:26:08,040
I'll keep the flame on low.

361
00:26:08,040 --> 00:26:11,050
You most likely have pots and pans
in a cabinet there.

362
00:26:11,050 --> 00:26:14,190
Here is your bag.

363
00:26:14,190 --> 00:26:16,730
Your wallet is in there, too.

364
00:26:16,730 --> 00:26:19,180
Then I'll be heading out.

365
00:26:21,280 --> 00:26:23,260
Hey, wait.

366
00:26:23,260 --> 00:26:26,340
- You're leaving?
- Why would I stay?

367
00:26:26,340 --> 00:26:29,420
You live here, by yourself I presume.

368
00:26:29,420 --> 00:26:32,860
Women live alone?

369
00:26:32,860 --> 00:26:35,120
Yeah. It's not so unheard of nowadays.

370
00:26:35,120 --> 00:26:38,510
Especially since both men and women work.

371
00:26:47,400 --> 00:26:51,930
Ms. Nashiki and I
are both elementary school teachers.

372
00:26:55,600 --> 00:26:57,050
[Third Grade Math]

373
00:26:59,320 --> 00:27:01,940
[Third Grade Math]

374
00:27:11,410 --> 00:27:14,380
<i>Confucius says, they who know the truth...</i>

375
00:27:14,380 --> 00:27:15,410
Sorry!

376
00:27:15,410 --> 00:27:19,680
<i>are not equal to those who love it,
and they who love it</i>

377
00:27:19,680 --> 00:27:22,480
<i>are not equal to those who delight in it.</i>

378
00:27:22,480 --> 00:27:29,480
Those who are keen to learn,
excel over those with knowledge.

379
00:27:32,960 --> 00:27:39,410
And those who find joy in learning,
rise above them all.

380
00:27:40,060 --> 00:27:43,790
In other words, I am talking about her!

381
00:27:45,160 --> 00:27:48,100
This is Iroha, of the Tokubei Couriers.

382
00:27:48,100 --> 00:27:52,350
Her passion for math triumphs that
of some of our smartest warriors.

383
00:27:52,350 --> 00:27:54,160
Master Seki, that is a bit...

384
00:27:54,160 --> 00:27:56,620
But master, she is a commoner!

385
00:27:56,620 --> 00:27:58,190
A commoner, besting a warrior?

386
00:27:58,190 --> 00:28:00,850
Education has no such boundaries.

387
00:28:00,850 --> 00:28:03,130
Then can you solve this question?

388
00:28:04,900 --> 00:28:09,890
Let's see. "There are a total of
60 pheasants and rabbits,

389
00:28:09,890 --> 00:28:12,500
with a total count of 150 feet."

390
00:28:12,500 --> 00:28:16,210
Ah, the pheasant and rabbit problem.

391
00:28:16,210 --> 00:28:20,250
You would use an area calculation for this.

392
00:28:20,250 --> 00:28:22,020
First and foremost...

393
00:28:24,040 --> 00:28:30,330
Is this a booklet teaching mathematics?

394
00:28:30,330 --> 00:28:32,160
Yes.

395
00:28:32,790 --> 00:28:34,420
We just call it math nowadays.

396
00:28:34,420 --> 00:28:38,370
And this is beginners mathematics.
They teach this to young warriors.

397
00:28:38,370 --> 00:28:39,760
Makes sense.

398
00:28:39,760 --> 00:28:44,530
An elementary school teacher
would teach to a similar age range.

399
00:28:47,550 --> 00:28:50,540
- What?
- Can it be...

400
00:28:50,540 --> 00:28:53,150
Can it really be?

401
00:28:53,150 --> 00:28:56,700
Master Seki, is that for your next lecture?

402
00:28:56,700 --> 00:28:58,610
May I see?

403
00:29:02,050 --> 00:29:04,690
You truly are passionate.

404
00:29:06,700 --> 00:29:08,250
My apologies, sir.

405
00:29:08,250 --> 00:29:12,180
- Why apologize?
- I have no need to look at such documents.

406
00:29:12,180 --> 00:29:15,880
Why? You love mathematics.

407
00:29:15,880 --> 00:29:17,380
Yes.

408
00:29:17,970 --> 00:29:22,700
However, liking the subject
won't change a thing.

409
00:29:22,700 --> 00:29:25,880
A young woman cannot open a school.

410
00:29:25,880 --> 00:29:28,050
People would never accept such a thing.

411
00:29:28,690 --> 00:29:32,100
Ah, the public opinion.

412
00:29:32,780 --> 00:29:35,830
But is that any reason
to give up your passion?

413
00:29:38,960 --> 00:29:45,700
Year 8 of the Keicho Era.
Before the Tokugawa brought peace to Edo,

414
00:29:45,700 --> 00:29:51,980
a period of war and bloodshed
lasted for 150 years.

415
00:29:51,980 --> 00:29:53,420
Yes.

416
00:29:54,310 --> 00:29:58,210
There is no such thing
as unchanging in this world.

417
00:29:59,790 --> 00:30:02,850
People change as do the seasons.

418
00:30:02,850 --> 00:30:06,330
And so do their values.

419
00:30:06,330 --> 00:30:08,980
But, Iroha.

420
00:30:10,650 --> 00:30:12,650
You mustn't change for them.

421
00:30:13,530 --> 00:30:18,150
Your fun and your enjoyment
is yours to keep.

422
00:30:18,930 --> 00:30:22,810
You must never give up
your love for mathematics.

423
00:30:23,520 --> 00:30:25,850
Devote yourself to it.

424
00:30:25,850 --> 00:30:29,160
One day, this world will change.

425
00:30:29,810 --> 00:30:31,620
So you'll fight on.

426
00:30:34,410 --> 00:30:38,780
Yes, Master Seki.

427
00:30:38,780 --> 00:30:46,410
Is this an age where women like me
can become teachers as well?

428
00:30:47,960 --> 00:30:53,760
Of course. They can become teachers
for children, teens, and young adults.

429
00:30:53,760 --> 00:30:56,000
Man or woman has nothing to do with it.

430
00:30:58,280 --> 00:31:00,120
I see.

431
00:31:03,590 --> 00:31:06,070
I see.

432
00:31:12,010 --> 00:31:16,020
However, Ms. Nashiki...

433
00:31:17,740 --> 00:31:20,530
I don't think she would have lasted long.

434
00:31:22,080 --> 00:31:25,590
She stayed at school
from dawn till almost midnight,

435
00:31:26,590 --> 00:31:29,220
and still had work left over.

436
00:31:35,800 --> 00:31:38,160
[1st Grade - Language Studies]

437
00:31:38,160 --> 00:31:40,520
[6th Grade - Sciences]

438
00:31:47,960 --> 00:31:50,240
These dates...

439
00:31:50,240 --> 00:31:51,440
No way.
[June, Week One]

440
00:31:51,440 --> 00:31:53,040
What?

441
00:31:54,960 --> 00:31:58,830
She made daily curriculums
for the entire year.

442
00:31:58,830 --> 00:32:00,690
For this subject.

443
00:32:00,690 --> 00:32:02,230
And this one, too.

444
00:32:02,230 --> 00:32:05,220
Did she have one planned for every grade?

445
00:32:06,310 --> 00:32:08,170
Is that amazing?

446
00:32:08,170 --> 00:32:09,500
It's beyond amazing.

447
00:32:09,500 --> 00:32:12,280
This amount of prep would take
an absurd amount of time.

448
00:32:12,280 --> 00:32:14,820
No one would normally put in
this amount of effort.

449
00:32:21,980 --> 00:32:24,040
Ms. Nashiki...

450
00:32:26,300 --> 00:32:28,990
She worked hard.

451
00:32:28,990 --> 00:32:33,110
She wanted to make sure
she was ready to teach,

452
00:32:33,110 --> 00:32:37,590
no matter what sort of
extra work came her way.

453
00:32:39,040 --> 00:32:43,950
She prepared so much, and yet...

454
00:32:44,680 --> 00:32:46,290
She crumbled.

455
00:32:46,290 --> 00:32:47,940
Why?

456
00:32:50,300 --> 00:32:54,120
Teaching nowadays is a strange job.

457
00:32:55,040 --> 00:32:59,600
Not only do we teach,
but we act as substitute parents as well.

458
00:32:59,600 --> 00:33:06,320
Things parents ought to teach their kids,
have now become our responsibility.

459
00:33:06,320 --> 00:33:09,720
<i>And if something goes wrong,
all the blame falls on the teacher.</i>

460
00:33:09,720 --> 00:33:14,440
<i>The principal, Board of Education,
board of directors, PTA...</i>

461
00:33:14,440 --> 00:33:17,190
<i>Us teachers can only nod,
and obey their orders.</i>

462
00:33:18,860 --> 00:33:23,260
We have to kill ourselves to work.

463
00:33:24,810 --> 00:33:27,120
What the hell?

464
00:33:28,050 --> 00:33:30,620
The absolute nerve!

465
00:33:31,510 --> 00:33:36,860
Teaching is not a job to look down on.

466
00:33:36,860 --> 00:33:42,400
Do you realize how special a role it is?

467
00:33:44,550 --> 00:33:48,630
Confucius teaches "Heaven, Earth,
Sovereign, Ancestors, and Teachers".

468
00:33:48,630 --> 00:33:52,050
They are five elements, in a balance.

469
00:33:52,050 --> 00:33:56,750
And we are taught to respect each as equals.

470
00:33:56,750 --> 00:33:59,570
When a parent
leaves their child with a teacher,

471
00:33:59,570 --> 00:34:05,240
they plead with all their might,
that you will guide them well.

472
00:34:05,240 --> 00:34:08,120
That is how important your role is.

473
00:34:08,120 --> 00:34:10,610
Discipline is to be taught by the parent.

474
00:34:10,610 --> 00:34:16,050
While teachers guide their students
to becoming proper adults.

475
00:34:16,700 --> 00:34:21,340
That is why teaching is such a special job.

476
00:34:21,950 --> 00:34:24,400
Status be damned.

477
00:34:24,400 --> 00:34:28,230
Everyone is to respect a teacher.

478
00:34:28,230 --> 00:34:31,490
How to praise or scold their child
is up to the teacher,

479
00:34:31,490 --> 00:34:33,500
the parents have no say in this.

480
00:34:33,500 --> 00:34:38,300
That is what it means
to entrust your child to a teacher.

481
00:34:39,980 --> 00:34:42,100
And yet, you.

482
00:34:42,940 --> 00:34:45,480
You're a coward.

483
00:34:45,480 --> 00:34:49,530
How can you be so complacent?
Stand your ground.

484
00:34:49,530 --> 00:34:52,430
Or don't you dare call yourself a teacher!

485
00:34:55,490 --> 00:34:57,650
What would you know?

486
00:34:59,900 --> 00:35:02,950
That just won't fly nowadays.

487
00:35:04,930 --> 00:35:07,440
A teacher paves the way for a kid's future.

488
00:35:07,980 --> 00:35:10,700
You think I don't know that?

489
00:35:10,700 --> 00:35:14,580
But teaching isn't the only job
we have at school.

490
00:35:14,580 --> 00:35:17,980
Miscellaneous work endlessly piles up on us.

491
00:35:18,740 --> 00:35:22,300
You think I'm so misguided, but I...

492
00:35:26,060 --> 00:35:28,380
I became a teacher because I wanted to.

493
00:35:28,380 --> 00:35:32,210
I wanted to become a teacher
who would leave an impression on kids.

494
00:35:34,760 --> 00:35:36,310
But...

495
00:35:38,700 --> 00:35:40,970
So did I.

496
00:35:42,270 --> 00:35:45,620
It was my lifelong dream.

497
00:35:53,030 --> 00:35:54,790
Can't I do it?

498
00:35:56,600 --> 00:36:02,800
Can't I take Iroha Nashiki's place,
and become a teacher?

499
00:36:02,800 --> 00:36:04,600
That'd be impossible.

500
00:36:04,600 --> 00:36:06,850
Our eras don't even use
the same alphabet.

501
00:36:06,850 --> 00:36:09,240
Then I'll just learn what I don't know.

502
00:36:09,840 --> 00:36:14,690
I'll become the teacher she wanted to be.

503
00:36:15,880 --> 00:36:20,830
Where is the original Ms. Nashiki?

504
00:36:20,830 --> 00:36:22,800
Beats me.

505
00:36:23,430 --> 00:36:26,600
I don't even know why I'm here myself.

506
00:36:27,390 --> 00:36:31,950
But if this is no dream,

507
00:36:31,950 --> 00:36:34,170
if this is really happening,

508
00:36:35,030 --> 00:36:37,990
then I've got no choice but to live on.

509
00:36:39,550 --> 00:36:43,080
Although, with how suddenly this happened,

510
00:36:43,620 --> 00:36:46,740
things might suddenly go back to normal, too.

511
00:36:46,740 --> 00:36:50,060
That's when Iroha Nashiki will return.

512
00:36:51,530 --> 00:36:53,220
Right back here.

513
00:36:58,530 --> 00:37:02,980
Can we wait a night first?

514
00:37:03,890 --> 00:37:10,460
This might all be a dream once we wake up.

515
00:37:12,260 --> 00:37:15,330
I'm going to head home.

516
00:37:15,330 --> 00:37:18,730
Make a cup of tea for yourself,
and get comfortable.

517
00:37:18,730 --> 00:37:20,330
Good night.

518
00:37:31,180 --> 00:37:34,690
<i>You made the right call.</i>

519
00:37:37,980 --> 00:37:43,040
<i>You decided, didn't you? Don't get involved.</i>

520
00:37:46,290 --> 00:37:49,010
Make tea?

521
00:37:55,900 --> 00:38:00,900
I think he pressed this for fire...

522
00:38:03,900 --> 00:38:05,660
A bit small, isn't it?

523
00:38:51,260 --> 00:38:52,940
I know.

524
00:38:58,970 --> 00:39:00,650
What do I do...

525
00:39:35,580 --> 00:39:37,220
Get back!

526
00:39:42,620 --> 00:39:44,080
Why?

527
00:39:44,080 --> 00:39:45,830
Now is not the time!

528
00:39:50,830 --> 00:39:53,240
- The fire!
- Damn it!

529
00:39:53,240 --> 00:39:55,190
Work with me here!

530
00:39:55,650 --> 00:39:57,090
Damn it...

531
00:40:55,500 --> 00:40:58,960
- This is awful.
- What the heck?

532
00:40:58,960 --> 00:41:02,110
This is awesome!

533
00:41:02,850 --> 00:41:06,220
Look! Wow...

534
00:41:06,220 --> 00:41:09,830
Like cherry blossom colored snowflakes!

535
00:41:12,080 --> 00:41:15,170
<i>What is her deal?</i>

536
00:41:15,170 --> 00:41:17,120
It sure is pretty!

537
00:41:23,200 --> 00:41:25,450
<i>She...</i>

538
00:41:25,450 --> 00:41:30,240
<i>She's having a blast despite my bad luck.</i>

539
00:41:33,830 --> 00:41:37,940
I'm sorry,
it's my fault for being in the way.

540
00:41:40,280 --> 00:41:42,820
[100 points - Amazing job!]

541
00:41:52,130 --> 00:41:54,400
- Here.
- Thank you.

542
00:42:13,440 --> 00:42:15,220
Thank goodness...

543
00:42:21,340 --> 00:42:23,630
Want to give it a try?

544
00:42:23,630 --> 00:42:26,500
All the textbooks and notes are safe.

545
00:42:26,500 --> 00:42:30,880
Maybe my unlucky streak doesn't affect you.

546
00:42:30,880 --> 00:42:33,750
What do you mean?

547
00:42:33,750 --> 00:42:35,540
Nothing.

548
00:42:36,500 --> 00:42:38,900
Make sure you grab all the notebooks.

549
00:42:45,190 --> 00:42:47,340
- Where are we going?
- My house.

550
00:42:47,340 --> 00:42:50,740
- Huh?
- You have nowhere else to go, right?

551
00:42:51,670 --> 00:42:55,150
And you're not well-versed
in our current world either.

552
00:42:56,620 --> 00:43:01,550
In any case, you must live your life
as "Ms. Nashiki" for now.

553
00:43:02,740 --> 00:43:09,010
Does that mean... I can be a teacher?

554
00:43:16,590 --> 00:43:20,570
I can't guarantee how long it'll last though.

555
00:43:20,570 --> 00:43:24,280
That's fine. I don't care.

556
00:43:24,280 --> 00:43:26,200
Until that end comes, I'll do it.

557
00:43:38,580 --> 00:43:40,220
Come on.

558
00:43:44,080 --> 00:43:46,550
<i>I've got absolutely rotten luck.</i>

559
00:43:46,550 --> 00:43:50,700
<i>I'm positive I'm the unluckiest guy around.</i>

560
00:43:50,700 --> 00:43:52,470
I'm leaving you behind.

561
00:43:52,470 --> 00:43:54,940
Give me time, will you?

562
00:44:14,920 --> 00:44:17,120
Come on, let's go.

563
00:44:17,120 --> 00:44:21,850
<i>But from now on, something might change.</i>

564
00:44:21,850 --> 00:44:26,130
- Do you know where we're going?
- Of course not.

565
00:44:32,890 --> 00:44:35,310
I'll call a taxi! Wait!

566
00:44:36,600 --> 00:44:39,540
Are you listening?

567
00:44:52,740 --> 00:44:55,090
Master Seki.

568
00:44:57,440 --> 00:45:00,060
Master Seki!

569
00:45:00,060 --> 00:45:01,970
Master Seki!

570
00:45:01,970 --> 00:45:03,360
What an invigorating presence!

571
00:45:03,360 --> 00:45:06,000
Master Seki! Welcome, welcome.

572
00:45:06,000 --> 00:45:09,930
I had requested Iroha
to assist with teaching today. Is she in?

573
00:45:09,930 --> 00:45:12,450
Huh? She isn't with you?

574
00:45:12,450 --> 00:45:15,760
- No.
- I wonder where she's wandered off to.

575
00:45:15,760 --> 00:45:18,510
I assumed she was with you, Master Seki.

576
00:45:18,510 --> 00:45:22,060
That fool. Where has she run off to?

577
00:45:22,060 --> 00:45:24,200
How strange...

578
00:45:24,200 --> 00:45:27,550
She would never break a promise.

579
00:45:50,990 --> 00:45:52,510
Iroha?
